rs between those countries  but of course if a country has very high  risk with rising infection rates and  poor controls then there be very tight  controls on anyone accessing the UK from  those markets I think that's the kind of  thing we should be thinking about for  the next phase in a month or so as time  as we start to see the infection rates  coming down in this country and we want  to start opening up the economy again  what if any what if any role do you see  for the the antibody test that we're  hearing much more about these days a  test that would be able to indicate  whether or not someone had previously  had covered 19 are you of a mind that we  could see something that I've seen  described as an immunity passport  brought in I think those are the kind of  things which  to be looking at but again we need to  have some consistency between countries  as to how this works  there's no good having the UK having a  health passport if another country has  the entirely different system well you  really need to do is to have that  commonality between markets so that you  know that if you've got your health  passport that is going to be accepted in  the country you are going to and you'll  be allowed to return home safely without  having any kind of quarantine or risk of  of being locked up at any part of your  journey so I think antibody testing the  testing for whether you have the the  illness they have a role to play and but  we probably need to get to a point where  the science is a little bit more certain  and the tests are a little bit quicker  before those can really be effective but  this is where we need to work with the  government and with other governments to  make sure that there is this common  international standard but I think I  think testing in a number of ways could  be very powerful one thing we are doing  at Ypres is testing thermal imaging  which we know has its limitations it is  very widely used in many other countries  as being the main 